# Monitor: x86 direct perf (Intel + AMD EPYC)

**Activation:** `alwaysApply: false` — when triggers in `agent-discipline-core.mdc` match, **Read this file before editing**. Triggered rules are mandatory, not optional.

Since **3.1 Phases 2+3** (cpu-precision plan, D5 / D19 / D20) the monitor links **no LIKWID on any
architecture**. x86 core, uncore and RAPL counters are read by direct `perf_event_open` on the
shared `perf_util` helper, the same model as Grace (**`monitor-arm-direct-perf.mdc`**, whose laws
1–4 and 9–11 this rule applies to x86). LIKWID (`likwid_*`, `cpu_counter_metrics_likwid_begin*`,
`HPMinit` / `setupCounters` re-arm, `LIKWID_FORCE`, `HPCPERFSTATS_LIKWID_ACCESS`, PWR* eventsets,
`--with-cpu-counter-backend=likwid`) must not come back. `LIKWID_MIGRATION.md` is history only.

## Modules

| Module | Role |
|--------|------|
| `x86_core_perf.{c,h}` | `host_cpu_hw` core events: one fd per event per CPU on the sysfs `cpu` PMU (no groups). Class from `processor_t`; generic `instructions` / `cpu-cycles` / `ref-cycles` from sysfs (else `PERF_TYPE_HARDWARE`), every other event from a per-class raw row (`config = umask << 8 \| event`). Software lifetimes that only grow; D6 time sums. |
| `cpu_counter_metrics_x86_publish.{c,h}` | `publish_x86_measured_stats`: lifetime → key table (one event may feed two keys), Zen5 `amd_fp_dp_ops` = packed + scalar double, D6 `pmu_*` keys. |
| `cpu_counter_metrics.c` (x86 branch) | begin = `x86_core_perf_begin` (startup / SIGHUP), collect = `x86_core_perf_collect_cpu` + publish, then `stats_set_missing_all(NOT_PRESENT)`. |
| `x86_uncore_perf.{c,h}` | Intel IMC (`uncore_imc_<N>` → `mbox<N>`, ICX `mdev<N>`), SPR / EMR HBM (`uncore_hbm_<N>` → `hbm<N>`), AMD DF (`amd_df` → `df`). Per-generation profiles; sysfs `events/` names first, else verified raw `event` / `umask` placed through the PMU's `format/` files (multi-range fields included). One fd per (event, cpumask CPU); the row sums every cpumask CPU. |
| `intel_{skx,icx,spr,emr,gnr,srf}_imc.c`, `amd_x86_uncore_df.c` | Thin collectors: `x86_uncore_perf_begin` with the CPUID match, `x86_uncore_perf_collect`. |
| `x86_rapl.{c,h}`, `rapl_powercap.{c,h}` | Per-socket RAPL: perf `power` PMU `energy-*` (scaled by `.scale`) on the PMU cpumask CPU of that socket, else powercap `energy_uj` for that domain (chosen at begin). |
| `intel_rapl.c`, `amd64_rapl.c` | `intel_x86_rapl` (`pkg/pp0/pp1/dram_energy`) and `amd_x86_rapl` (`core/pkg_energy`) on `x86_rapl`. |

## Laws

1. **No LIKWID, no MSR, no fallback collectors.** Never `msr_io`, `/dev/cpu/*/msr`, `power_read`,
   Intel `0x611…` / AMD `0xC001029A/B`, `intel_x86_pmc_gpr4` / `gpr8`, `intel_4pmc3` /
   `intel_8pmc3`, AMD `amd64_pmc`, native-MSR `amd64_df`, or emitted `amd_x86_pmc` / bare
   `amd_x86_uncore_df`. A missing PMU or failed open fails **soft** (law 7), never closed.
2. **Scaled reads only, never `pinned` / `exclusive`.** Every fd uses
   `PERF_FORMAT_TOTAL_TIME_ENABLED | PERF_FORMAT_TOTAL_TIME_RUNNING` and goes through
   `perf_read_scaled` (arm law 1). The kernel multiplexes against any other holder (`perf`,
   VTune, a user's LIKWID job, `nvidia-powerd`); exact while an event keeps a counter, a scaled
   estimate otherwise. No per-tick re-arm, no rotation, no reprogramming in collect.
3. **Opens at begin / SIGHUP only.** Collect is one bounded `read` per open fd
   (`monitor-jitter-and-fidelity-priority.mdc`). PMU `type` and `cpumask` are read every begin,
   never hard-coded; no assumption on IMC / HBM instance count (`X86_UNCORE_MAX_DEVS`).
4. **Verified event rows only.** A raw code enters a class row only after it was checked with
   `perf` against the kernel's named event (FLOP codes also against an exact GEMM FLOP total,
   `scripts/perf_raw_flops_check.sh`) on that class. Never guess codes; SRF and Rome have no core
   row and fall under "unknown class" (generic events only, one log line, other keys
   `NOT_PRESENT`). Current rows:
   - Intel SKX / CLX / ICX / SPR / EMR / GNR: `0x51/0x01` `l1d_replacement`; **D18** `0xd1/0x01`,
     `0x02`, `0x04` → `mem_load_uops_retired_l1_hit` / `_l2_hit` / `_llc_hit` on every class
     (the LIKWID alias that put all loads into `_l1_hit` on ICX+ is gone); the eight
     `0xc7` `fp_arith_inst_retired_*` umasks `0x01`–`0x80`.
   - AMD Milan / Genoa (Zen3 / Zen4): `0xc0`, `0xc2`, `0xc3`, `0x29/0x07` (`ls_dispatch`),
     `0x03/0xff` → `fp_ops_retired` (D10). Turin (Zen5): same plus `0x03/0x0f` →
     `fp_ops_retired`, `0x03/0xaf` + `0x03/0x8f` → `amd_fp_dp_ops` (D12). Event `0x03` is a
     merged counter pair (`pair_event`, counts two general slots).
   - Uncore: Intel CAS per generation (SKX `0x04/0x03,0x0c`; ICX `0x04/0x0f,0x30`; SPR / EMR / GNR /
     SRF `0x05/0xcf,0xf0`, GNR / SRF scheduler 0 only); SPR HBM CAS (32-byte accesses, D21);
     AMD DF Zen2 / Zen3 `dram_channel_data_controller_N` (Milan channels 0–7, checked against
     the read load's bytes on c317-016, campaign finding 26; Rome channels 0–1 only), Zen4 / Zen5
     read and write beats per channel (channels 0–3; 4–11 wait for a Genoa check).
   - **AMD DF is one `chan<N>` row per DRAM channel** (rows-split class, `n_rows` 8 Rome / Milan,
     12 Genoa / Turin), never one key per channel and never channels folded together: Zen2 /
     Zen3 `dram_data_beats` (combined), Zen4 / Zen5 `dram_read_beats` / `dram_write_beats`. A
     row with no verified event emits `NOT_BUILT`.
5. **Keys, widths and vendor gating.** Existing key names stay (no renames; the one exception,
   AMD DF `dram_chanN_bytes` -> per-row beat keys, went through the consumer migration). Shared counter keys
   are `W=48` and carry the software lifetime **modulo 2^48** (O11); `fp_ops_retired`,
   `amd_fp_dp_ops` and the `pmu_*` times are `W=64`. Generic `instructions` → `instr_retired_any` +
   `instr_retired`, `cycles` → `cycles_unhalted_core` + `aperf`, `ref-cycles` →
   `cycles_unhalted_ref` + `mperf`. On AMD (`is_amd`) the Intel fixed names
   (`instr_retired_any`, `cycles_unhalted_core`, `cycles_unhalted_ref`) and `mperf` are left
   unset (`NOT_PRESENT`; Genoa has no `ref-cycles`), while `instr_retired` / `aperf` are filled.
   Uncore counted keys are CAS / data-beat counts modulo 2^48; RAPL keys are `W=32` running totals
   in mJ since begin, published modulo 2^32 (never a per-read window).
6. **Bridge-safe accumulators.** Same shape as `arm_core_perf`: `life += (delta + 0.5)` only when
   the scaled delta is > 0; a failed read keeps `prev` so the next delta spans the gap; a re-seed
   adds 0. Never publish a raw kernel count. `test_x86_core_perf_bridge` locks this; the
   `x86perf` capability token tells consumers the x86 `host_cpu_hw` chain is bridgeable.
7. **Fail soft: the key emits its sentinel** (`monitor-value-sentinel-contract.mdc`). Open errno
   maps through `perf_open_sentinel` (`EACCES`/`EPERM` → `PERMISSION_DENIED`,
   `ENOENT`/`EINVAL`/`EOPNOTSUPP` or no row → `NOT_PRESENT`, `EBUSY` → `PMU_BUSY`); a failed read
   is `READ_FAILED`, `Δrunning == 0` with `Δenabled > 0` is `NOT_SCHEDULED`, a re-seed is
   `COUNTER_RESET`. An uncore key summed over several fds emits its sentinel rather than a
   partial sum. A key no event of the row feeds is `NOT_PRESENT`, or `NOT_BUILT` when the
   profile lists it in `not_built` (SKX `dram_act_count` / `dram_pre_count_miss`) or another row
   of the class feeds it (DF rows of Rome channels 2–7, Genoa / Turin channels 4–11). One log
   line per event (not per CPU). Every failure site goes through
   `stats_set_missing`, so the fault-injection matrix reaches it.
8. **D6 sharing keys on x86.** `host_cpu_hw` publishes `pmu_enabled_time`, `pmu_running_time`
   and (with a class row) `pmu_solo_running_time`; every IMC / HBM / DF row publishes
   `pmu_enabled_time` / `pmu_running_time` (`E,W=64,U=ns`, summed over the row's fds; no solo for
   uncore). Solo: fixed-counter events (Intel generic events) run all the time; general events get
   min(1, `gp_counters` ÷ open general slots). Intel `gp_counters` = 8 assumes **HT off** (true on
   every measured node; with HT on there are 4 and solo is overstated). The kernel NMI watchdog
   holds Intel's fixed cycles counter, so `cycles` multiplexes and shows as outside sharing on
   nodes with `nmi_watchdog=1`; do not "fix" that by pinning.
9. **Uncore socket coverage.** A row sums every CPU of its PMU's `cpumask` (one per socket or
   die), so Intel IMC rows count **all sockets** (about 2× the per-socket LIKWID history on
   2-socket nodes). When a cpumask names fewer CPUs than the node has sockets (el8 `amd_df`
   counts socket 0 only, D13) begin logs it once; do not add an MSR workaround.
10. **RAPL.** Probe the perf `power` PMU `events/energy-{pkg,cores,gpu,ram}` (with `.scale`,
    Joules) per socket, then powercap zones (`package-N` / `core` / `uncore` / `dram`); program
    only present domains. A domain neither source can read emits its sentinel every collect; a
    type with no readable domain on any socket is disabled with one error line. Do not gate RAPL
    on `MONITOR_ARCH_*`. Flat RAPL rows under load are a failure, not idle.
11. **Family-named types, server CPUID ranges.** DF `st_name`s are
    `amd_x86_uncore_df_{rome,milan,genoa,turin}`, IMC `intel_x86_uncore_imc_{skx,icx,spr,emr,gnr,srf}`
    (ICX rows keep `mdev<N>` names). EPYC allowlists are server model ranges only
    (`amd_cpuid_match.c`).
12. **Tests use the fake perf layer** (`tests/test_fake_perf.{c,h}`: TMPDIR sysfs + pipe fds) and
    run on every host: `test_x86_core_perf`, `test_x86_core_perf_bridge`,
    `test_cpu_counter_x86_publish`, `test_x86_uncore_perf`, `test_x86_rapl`, `test_rapl_powercap`,
    the IMC / DF schema tests and the `test_fault_inject_*` matrix. New event rows need a test row
    and the hardware evidence cited in the plan.

## Live healthy bar (x86 canary)

Unit `active`, shm age &lt; 300 s; `@full` has `host_cpu_hw`, the node's IMC or DF type and a
non-flat RAPL type. Judge `host_cpu_hw` under load on `instr_retired` / `aperf` (both vendors),
`retired_instructions` / `ls_dispatch` (AMD) or `cycles_unhalted_core` (Intel); idle CPUs may stay
flat. `pmu_running_time` ÷ `pmu_enabled_time` well below the solo share means another PMU user.

